  "body": "The maintainer reported this in chat on 2026-11-15, on the v0.21.0 tree. Related: #1963 (the mill envelope is centred on the table plane), #1567 (the envelope display).\n\nWhat happens: for a lathe, the viewport draws the work envelope from the bottom up, as for a mill.\n\nExpected: the work space of a lathe is a cylinder along the spindle axis. In the usual view the spindle axis goes from left to right. The tool moves in two axes only: Z along the spindle and X across it. The envelope shows the Z travel along the spindle axis and the X travel (or the swing) as the radius.\n\nThe maintainer's words: \"work envelope for a lathe shows up as bottom and up.. The cylinder space of a lathe is in the left to right direction.. Tooling is traveling in left and right up and down - its just two axis right..\"\n\nQuestions for the work:\n\n- Find how the envelope display reads the machine type. Find if it uses the mill X/Y/Z box for a lathe machine file.\n- Decide the shape from the lathe machine data: Z travel and X travel, and the swing if the machine file has it. Record the choice in `docs/decisions/`.\n- Find if a travel-limit check for a lathe uses the same wrong frame (`crates/post/src/machine/envelope.rs`). If a check uses it, the defect is under the safety floor: raise the effort label to `effort:high` and say so here.\n- A lathe with a Y axis or live tools is not in scope. File a follow-up issue if it needs a different shape.\n\nDone means:\n\n- For a lathe machine file, the drawn envelope is along the spindle axis and starts at the chuck face or the Z travel limits of the machine file.\n- A test pins the axis and the extents of the lathe envelope (a structural or value test, not a pixel snapshot).\n- A comment here says if a check used the wrong frame, with the result.\n",
